[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router4
diagnosis_text:
The failure is on router4 (Location C gateway), not router3. Evidence: when PC11 (192.168.200.11) pings Location A (172.16.10.4), the reply is “Destination Net Unreachable” from 192.168.200.1 — router4’s eth0 IP — proving the default gateway lacks a route/uplink for those prefixes. On router4, only eth0 (192.168.200.1/24) is up; all upstream interfaces are down: eth5 (172.25.1.1/24, the link to router3) and eth4 (192.168.15.2/24, the link toward the rest of the core) both show state DOWN at the Linux level. FRR on router4 consequently has no RIP-learned routes; its routing table contains only the connected 192.168.200.0/24. This explains why Location C clients can reach nothing beyond their subnet and receive immediate ICMP net-unreachable.

Router3 is healthy on the C-facing side: its eth5 (172.25.1.2) is UP, and its routing table contains a path to 192.168.200.0/24 via 172.25.1.1 (router4). The failure mechanism is loss of L3 adjacency due to router4’s uplink interfaces being down, not a router3 drop.

Root cause: router4’s uplink interfaces (eth5 to router3 and eth4 to the core) are administratively/physically down, isolating Location C.

Corrective fix: restore router4 connectivity — bring up eth5 and eth4 (no-shutdown/ip link set up), verify cabling on link_r4_r3 and link_r4_r5, and confirm RIP adjacencies/route propagation. Do not replace router3; if hardware swap is needed, replace or repair router4’s faulty/disabled interfaces. 
=== END ===